.TH CCOPEN 1 "2026-11-22" "atelier instruments" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ccopen \- read what a session is opening onto, and whether anything is owing
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ccopen
.RB [ \-\-root
.IR dir ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-fetch ]
.br
.B ccopen
.BR \-h " | " \-\-help
.SH DESCRIPTION
A session that opens onto a repo is meant to sync first, look for other live
sessions, notice an interrupted predecessor's leavings, land any hand-up pull
requests, check its atelier pin, read the newest session files, wait for a
quiet machine, and skip what waits on the principal.
Those steps are prose, in
.IR CLAUDE.md ,
in the session-open prompt and in the recovery sweep of
.IR CONCURRENCY.md .
A session that narrates them from habit can skip one without anyone seeing.
.PP
.B ccopen
runs them as code, from a fresh read of the repository, the host and GitHub:
nine reads in a fixed order, then one verdict line.
It is the twin of
.BR ccclose (1),
which does the same for the close, and it reuses that tool's reads and
.BR cclive (1)'s
rather than copying them.
.PP
.B ccopen
is a command the session-open prompt runs, never a hook.
The principal uses the prompt only when he thinks it useful, and some sessions
purposely leave some of its commands out.
Nothing wires
.B ccopen
to session start, so a session opened without the prompt never runs it.
.SS The reads
Each read ends in one of four states:
.B \[u2705]
ready,
.B \[u26A0]
look,
.B \[u274C]
recover first, and
.B \[u2796]
not applicable, which means its input is absent and the line says why.
A read never reports a pass for something it could not read: an unreadable
input is
.B \[u26A0]
unknown, with the reason.
Every run prints the same fields in the same order, known zeros included.
Lists are capped at five per kind, with the cut counted.
.TP
.B Kind of session
Atelier itself (a
.I tools/pins.py
beside
.IR docs/method/PROPAGATION.md );
a child of atelier (a pin
.I atelier@<sha>
or the floor stamp in
.IR CLAUDE.md ),
naming the pin and its queue file;
a repo without doctrine, which is
.B \[u26A0]
because the prompt's minimum viable version applies, and which names the queue
file it found (\fIdocs/ROADMAP.md\fR or \fIROADMAP.md\fR) or says there is none;
or no repo at all.
.TP
.B Sync
The branch and head, the working-tree files changed (listed), and ahead and
behind against the branch's upstream, else the remote's default branch.
A dirty tree is
.B \[u26A0]
and says that someone else may be editing: take a worktree, never autostash.
A branch behind a clean tree is
.B \[u26A0]
with the pull command, \fBgit pull \-\-rebase\fR, to run on a clean tree only.
Unpushed commits, a diverged branch, a detached HEAD and a failed fetch warn
too, each in words.
.TP
.B Live work
.BR cclive 's
read, for this repo alone: transcripts written in the last ten minutes whose
working directory is in it, the \fB[~]\fR claims matched to them (by the
session id or worktree the claim stamp names), and the worktrees they are in.
The calling session
.RB ( $CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID )
is left out, so it never reports itself.
Any other live session, live claim or live worktree is
.BR \[u26A0] :
coordinate, take a worktree, skip an item with a live claim.
A repo with no board prints its claims as unknown, never zero.
.TP
.B Interrupted predecessor
The recovery sweep, run on every open and not only on its tell: a session that
died mid-flight leaves a last commit, then silence, and no closing session
file, but a tool cannot read silence, and the sweep is cheap.
It reports what no live session explains: dated claims no live session or
worktree matches (an undated \fB[~]\fR is counted and not judged, because its
stamp was not found), linked worktrees other than this one that no live session
is in, each with its changed-file count, local branches that are not the
default, not this one, not held by a worktree and not the head of an open pull
request, each marked merged or not merged, and stash entries.
Any of them is
.B \[u274C]
recover first.
The number of commits on the integration branch after the newest commit to the
session folder is printed as context and drives no state: in a repo with
parallel sessions it is nonzero most of the time.
.TP
.B Hand-up PRs
Open pull requests whose head ref starts
.I report\-
or
.IR handup/ ,
or whose title says hand-up (needs a GitHub remote and a working
.BR gh (1)).
In atelier itself, any is
.BR \[u274C] :
a child's filed finding has no owner until atelier lands it.
In another repo it is
.BR \[u26A0] ,
since landing is atelier's.
The count of other open pull requests is printed.
.B gh
missing, not authenticated or failing is
.B \[u26A0]
unknown.
.TP
.B Pin currency
.BR ccclose 's
pin check, unchanged, for a child: atelier's
.B tools/pins.py \-\-json \-\-child
on the repo.
A pin behind with the floor text unchanged is
.B \[u26A0]
(read the delta, then decide).
A floor that has changed since the pin, a floor copy that differs, or a pin
that is ahead, diverged or unknown is
.BR \[u274C] .
It is not applicable for atelier itself and for a repo with no pin.
.TP
.B Newest session files
The newest three names in the session folder
.RI ( docs/sessions/
or
.IR sessions/ )
at the integration branch, newest first, so the session reads them.
It names files and reads none.
A repo that keeps only the retired one-file index is
.B \[u26A0]
and says to read it by hand.
.TP
.B Machine load
The 1-, 5- and 15-minute load averages against the core count, to decide
whether to start now.
The machine is
.B busy
while the 5- or the 15-minute average is at or over the threshold, and quiet
only when both are under it, so a momentary dip is still busy and a burst
(the 1-minute average) alone judges nothing.
Busy is
.BR \[u26A0] :
don't start, re-check in
.B $CCOPEN_RECHECK_MIN
minutes (default 30, long enough for the 15-minute average to turn over
twice).
The threshold is
.B $CCOPEN_MAX_LOAD
when set to a positive number, else the core count (a load per core of 1.0 is
as much runnable work as the CPUs can run), and the line says which applied.
The JSON always carries
.IR load1 ,
.IR load5 ,
.IR load15 ,
.IR cores ,
.IR threshold ,
.I threshold_source
and
.IR busy .
In the verdict line a busy machine reads
.BR "machine busy" .
.TP
.B Waiting on the principal
On a per-item board (one file per item under
.IR docs/roadmap/ ),
read at the integration branch: the open items marked
.B \[u1F3AF]
on their state line (waiting on the principal), how many of them are parked,
and how many are not.
A parked item is an open item with a line beginning
.B \[u1F4A4] PARKED YYYY\-MM\-DD
(the principal was asked and said not now, undecided or hold; his words follow
on that line; the newest such line is the park).
A park has
.B changed
when, after the commit that added its newest line, a later commit touched the
item's own file or the file of an item it cites as a backticked
.IR NNN/NNN .
Those parks are listed (up to five, the cut counted) with what changed, and
the read is
.B \[u26A0]
.RI ( "parked items changed since you parked them: look" ).
An unchanged park is skipped, not re-asked.
Unparked
.B \[u1F3AF]
items are counted and not listed: a close puts only its own session's
questions (old asks are not put; board 180/250).
Without a per-item board the read is
.BR \[u2796] ,
and says why.
.SS The verdict
The last line is one of
.B "\[u2705] ready to start" ,
.B "\[u26A0] look first: ..."
or
.B "\[u274C] recover first: ..." ,
the last two naming each read that raised them in words.
Recover first wins when both apply.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-root " dir"
The repo to read.
Default: the current directory, resolved to its git top level.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Machine-readable output: the same reads, every field present on every run, and
the verdict as
.IR status ,
.IR text ,
.I recover
and
.IR look .
Lists carry up to 25 entries.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-fetch
Do not run
.B git fetch
in the repo.
The sync read is then as of the last fetch, and says so.
The pin read is measured offline, against the atelier checkout's local HEAD
(\fBpins.py \-\-head\fR), and says so.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
A one-screen summary.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
No read is recover-first.
Some may warn or be unknown; the verdict line says so.
.TP
.B 1
At least one read is recover-first.
.TP
.B 2
A usage error: an unknown flag, a flag with no value, or a
.B \-\-root
that is not a directory.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.claude/projects/*/*.jsonl
Claude Code's transcripts, whose modification times and working directories
the live-work read uses (as
.B cclive
does).
Read a bounded tail at a time, and only when fresh.
.TP
.I docs/ROADMAP.md, ROADMAP.md
The board, read for claims at the newest of the repo's
.IR main / master
refs, local and remote.
.TP
.I docs/sessions/, sessions/
The session folder, read at the integration branch.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.B ccopen
reuses
.BR ccclose 's
reads, so it honours the same test seams.
.TP
.B CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID
The calling session, left out of the live-work read and not charged with its
own claims.
Unset, nothing is left out and the sync and live reads say so.
.TP
.B CCOPEN_MAX_LOAD
The machine-load threshold: a positive number the 5- and 15-minute load
averages are compared against.
Default: the core count.
.TP
.B CCOPEN_RECHECK_MIN
How many minutes a session waits before it runs
.B ccopen
again on a busy machine: a positive number.
Default: 30.
Set it where the session sees it, such as the
.B env
block of the Claude Code settings.
.TP
.B CCOPEN_LOADAVG
Test seam: three comma-separated numbers, \fI1m,5m,15m\fR, standing in for the
real load averages.
.TP
.B CCOPEN_CORES
Test seam: a whole number standing in for the core count.
.TP
.B CCCLOSE_GH
The
.B gh
executable to run.
Default:
.BR gh .
The tests point it at a stub.
.TP
.B CCCLOSE_ATELIER
The atelier checkout the pin read measures against, and whose
.I tools/pins.py
it runs.
If set it is used as given: a wrong value is "no result", not a fall-back.
.TP
.B CCCLOSE_PINS_TIMEOUT
The pin read's time limit, in seconds.
Default: 20.
.TP
.B ATELIER_ESTATE_ROOT
Where the pin read looks for an
.I atelier
checkout when it has no better answer.
.TP
.B HOME
Where the transcripts are found.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B ccopen
The reads and the verdict, from the repo you are in, at session open.
.TP
.B ccopen \-\-no\-fetch
The same, with no network write; the counts are as of the last fetch.
.TP
.B ccopen \-\-root ~/.pets/atelier \-\-json | jq .verdict
The verdict, for a tool.
.SH NOTES
.B ccopen
is read-only, with one exception: it runs
.B git fetch
in the repo, says so on its
.B Fetch
line, and
.B \-\-no\-fetch
skips it.
A fetch updates remote-tracking refs and touches no working tree.
It never commits, pulls, removes a worktree or branch, drops a stash or edits
a file.
Every other git call runs with
.BR \-\-no\-optional\-locks ,
because a plain
.B git status
refreshes the index under
.IR index.lock ,
and on a checkout another session shares, that write can collide with its
commit.
The pin read also fetches, inside
.BR pins.py ,
in the atelier checkout (not in the repo read), unless
.B \-\-no\-fetch
is given.
.PP
.B "No live session matched is not proof that none exists."
A session open and waiting on its operator writes nothing, so a quiet claim,
worktree or branch can still belong to a live one, and a claim whose stamp
falls outside the first lines of its item is read as undated.
Another machine's sessions are invisible.
So a recover-first on residue means: read it, and settle it on the evidence
before new work.
It never means the residue is yours to delete: an orphan is reclaimed on the
evidence and never on a timer, and another session's recovery is not yours to
run.
.PP
The load read judges the machine, not a session: it cannot see which process
is heavy, and a machine quiet at the start can be busy a minute later, so it
says nothing about a run already under way.
The parked-change read counts any later commit that touches the item's file or
a cited item's file, so a sweep that edits many items wakes many parks, and an
item renumbered since it was parked reads as unplaced.
A \fB\[u1F4A4] PARKED\fR line quoted inside a code fence is not a park.
.PP
A cold reviewer does not run
.BR ccopen .
The session log is on the review rules' barred list, and the interrupted and
session-file reads look at the record.
.PP
It does not read the principal's own words for the run, the contents of the
files it names, or the reflog after the last close, and it cannot say whether
a dirty tree is a predecessor's or a peer's.
The design of record is
.IR instruments/ccopen.design.md .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ccclose (1),
.BR cclive (1),
.BR gh (1),
.BR git\-worktree (1),
